      "text": "**Scope:** `rankAssignments` and `applyDecision` only.\n\n## `rankAssignments`: no confirmed bug\n\n- **DP keyed by robot mask.** Each offer adds at most one pick, because it extends the old `states` and writes to `next`. A robot is never reused, because of the `mask&(1<<bid.robot)` check. Future choices depend only on the mask, so keeping the best state per mask is sufficient.\n- **Objective order.** `better` ranks by recirculated picks, then immediate pick count, then score. This matches the contract: recirculated priority first, then immediate count, then score.\n- **Soft deferral.**\n  - `future` uses `Math.max(-Infinity, …)`, so a box with no soft bid keeps all its eligible bids.\n  - A tie goes to immediate pickup (`>=`).\n  - Boxes with `passes>=1` never fall back to a soft bid.\n  - Soft winners do not enter the mask, so they consume no robot slot.\n\n## `applyDecision`: one confirmed issue\n\n### 1. Soft-downstream (`defer`) proposals can reserve the box and launch a motion\n\n```ts\nconst failures=[], action=interceptAction(s,w,proposal,...);\n...\nif(action){ b.status='reserved'; b.owner=proposal.robot; next.push(...); w.revision++; }\n```\n\n`proposal.disposition` is consulted only in the rejection branch. It is never consulted before `interceptAction` or before the reservation.\n\nWhen a `defer` proposal reaches apply time, `interceptAction` may succeed. For example, time has advanced and the box is now within the pickup window. The code then:\n\n- sets the box to `reserved`,\n- assigns the robot as owner,\n- pushes a motion.\n\nThis breaks two parts of the contract:\n\n- \"Soft downstream does not reserve capacity.\"\n- `launchNow=false` means not an immediate pickup.\n\nIt also undermines the DP's guarantee. `rankAssignments` deliberately leaves the deferred robot's slot free for another box. Proposals are processed in order, and nothing in this function updates `cell.phase` after a reservation. So the deferred box can claim the robot first, and the DP-chosen immediate box for that robot is then rejected or diverted. That happens only if `interceptAction`'s path-reservation check blocks it; otherwise both launches are attempted on the same robot.\n\n**Minimal fix:** for `disposition==='defer'`:\n\n- record the check and reason,\n- do not call `interceptAction` for reservation (or ignore its result),\n- `continue` without reserving or rejecting.\n\n```ts\nif(proposal.disposition==='defer'){\n  b.flow!.checks[proposal.robot]={version:proposal.cellVersion,at:w.time,reason:proposal.reason,blocked:proposal.blocked,tested:proposal.tested};\n  b.flow!.lastReason=proposal.reason;\n  continue;\n}\n```\n\n## Regression test\n\n**Setup**\n- Robot 0 has `phase='loading'`, and its version matches the decision.\n- Boxes A and B are both on the belt and are geometrically interceptable by robot 0 at `world.time`.\n- `decision.proposals` contains, in this order:\n  1. Box A, robot 0, `disposition:'defer'`.\n  2. Box B, robot 0, immediate (`launchNow=true` from `rankAssignments`).\n\n**Expect**\n- Box A: `status==='belt'`, `owner` unset, and not in `cells[0].rejected`.\n- Box B: `status==='reserved'` with `owner===0`.\n- Exactly one motion is pushed, with its action belonging to box B.\n- `world.revision` increments by exactly 1.\n\n**Current code**\n- Box A is reserved and launched on robot 0.\n- Box B then either fails reservation or is double-launched.\n\n**Single-proposal variant:** a lone feasible `defer` proposal should produce zero motions and an unchanged `revision`.\n\n## Not reported\n\nThese were considered but are not confirmed from the code shown:\n\n- **Pickup-window failure classification.** Whether a pickup-window failure triggers permanent `rejected` depends on the exact failure strings (whether they contain `구간`/`예약`). Those strings are not shown.\n- **Cycle check when `cellStates` is absent.** The per-proposal check compares phase but not cycle. This matters only if `cellStates` can legitimately be omitted, which the contract doesn't specify."
